CAT = "cpafarCurrent"; SEG = "CUR"
Q = [
("Which item qualifies as a cash equivalent?",
 "A Treasury bill bought 60 days before maturity", ["A Treasury bill bought 6 months before maturity", "A certificate of deposit due in one year", "Shares of a listed public company"],
 "Cash equivalents are short-term, highly liquid investments with original maturities of three months or less to the holder. Maturity is measured from the date the holder buys it."),
("A company has $85,000 in checking, $1,000 of petty cash, $40,000 in a money market fund, a $3,000 postdated check from a customer and a $20,000 nine-month certificate of deposit. Cash and cash equivalents are:",
 "$126,000", ["$129,000", "$146,000", "$86,000"],
 "$85,000 + $1,000 + $40,000 = $126,000. The postdated check is still a receivable, and the nine-month CD is a short-term investment, not a cash equivalent."),
("The bank statement shows $52,400. Deposits in transit are $6,100, outstanding checks are $8,300, and the bank wrongly charged this account $200 for another customer's check. The adjusted bank balance is:",
 "$50,400", ["$50,000", "$66,800", "$54,600"],
 "$52,400 + $6,100 - $8,300 + $200 = $50,400. The bank's error is added back on the bank side because the bank must correct it."),
("Which bank reconciliation item requires a journal entry on the company's books?",
 "A customer's NSF check", ["A deposit in transit", "An outstanding check", "A bank error on another account"],
 "Items the bank knows about but the company has not recorded, such as NSF checks and service charges, are booked by the company. Timing items and bank errors are fixed on the bank side."),
("The general ledger shows cash of $31,800. The bank charged a $60 fee, returned a $900 NSF check and collected a $4,000 note (including interest) for the company. The adjusted book balance is:",
 "$34,840", ["$36,760", "$35,800", "$30,840"],
 "$31,800 - $60 - $900 + $4,000 = $34,840. Leaving out the collected note gives $30,840."),
("Under ASC 326, the allowance for credit losses on trade receivables is based on:",
 "Expected losses over the asset's life", ["Probable losses already incurred", "Losses expected in the next year", "Actual write-offs as they occur"],
 "The current expected credit loss model records lifetime expected losses using past events, current conditions and reasonable forecasts. The incurred loss model it replaced waited for a probable loss."),
("An aging shows receivables of $300,000 current (1% expected loss), $80,000 at 31 to 60 days (5%) and $20,000 over 60 days (25%). The allowance has a $4,500 credit balance before adjustment. Credit loss expense is:",
 "$7,500", ["$12,000", "$16,500", "$4,500"],
 "Required allowance: $3,000 + $4,000 + $5,000 = $12,000. Expense brings the existing $4,500 up to that: $12,000 - $4,500 = $7,500."),
("Under the allowance method, writing off a specific uncollectible account causes:",
 "No change in net receivables", ["A decrease in net receivables", "A rise in credit loss expense", "An increase in net receivables"],
 "The write-off debits the allowance and credits accounts receivable by the same amount, so net receivables are unchanged. The expense was recognized when the allowance was built."),
("A customer pays an account the company had written off last year under the allowance method. The company should:",
 "Reinstate it, then record the cash", ["Credit a gain on recovery", "Credit sales revenue for the amount", "Debit the allowance again"],
 "The write-off is reversed (debit receivables, credit the allowance), and then the collection is recorded as usual. No gain or revenue arises."),
("A company sells 1,000 units at $50 each with a right of return and expects 4% to come back. Revenue recognized on the sale is:",
 "$48,000", ["$50,000", "$46,000", "$49,000"],
 "Under ASC 606, revenue excludes amounts expected to be refunded: $50,000 x 96% = $48,000. The $2,000 is a refund liability, with an asset for goods expected back."),
("A company factors $200,000 of receivables without recourse in a transfer that qualifies as a sale. The factor charges a 3% fee and holds back 5% for returns. The loss on the sale is:",
 "$6,000", ["$16,000", "$10,000", "$0"],
 "The fee of $6,000 is the loss. The $10,000 holdback is a receivable from the factor, not a loss, so cash received is $184,000."),
("A company transfers receivables but keeps effective control over them, so the sale conditions of ASC 860 are not met. The transfer is recorded as:",
 "A secured borrowing", ["A sale of receivables", "An exchange of assets", "A capital contribution"],
 "When a transfer fails the sale criteria, the receivables stay on the books and the cash received is a liability, as in a secured borrowing."),
("A company pledges its receivables as collateral for a bank loan. The receivables are:",
 "Kept on the books and disclosed", ["Removed from the books", "Reclassified as noncurrent assets", "Written down to the loan"],
 "Pledging does not transfer the receivables. They remain assets, the loan is a liability, and the pledge is disclosed."),
("Accounts receivable started at $90,000. Credit sales were $600,000, collections $570,000 and write-offs $8,000. Ending accounts receivable are:",
 "$112,000", ["$120,000", "$104,000", "$82,000"],
 "$90,000 + $600,000 - $570,000 - $8,000 = $112,000. Forgetting the write-offs gives $120,000."),
("The receivables subledger totals $210,000 and the general ledger $214,000. A $4,000 credit memo for a return was posted to the subledger only. The company should:",
 "Post the credit memo to the ledger", ["Raise the subledger by $4,000", "Book $4,000 of credit losses", "Leave the difference as is"],
 "The control account was never reduced for the return. Recording the credit memo in the general ledger brings it to $210,000 and agrees it with the subledger."),
("Beginning inventory was 100 units at $10. Purchases were 200 units at $12, then 100 units at $15. 250 units were sold. Ending inventory under periodic FIFO is:",
 "$2,100", ["$1,600", "$1,838", "$2,250"],
 "FIFO leaves the latest costs in ending inventory: 100 x $15 + 50 x $12 = $2,100. LIFO would leave $1,600 and weighted average about $1,838."),
("Beginning inventory was 50 units at $20. Purchases were 150 units at $22, then 100 units at $25. 180 units were sold. Cost of goods sold under periodic LIFO is:",
 "$4,260", ["$3,860", "$4,500", "$4,080"],
 "LIFO charges the latest costs first: 100 x $25 + 80 x $22 = $4,260. FIFO gives $3,860 and weighted average $4,080."),
("FIFO inventory costs $80,000. Its expected selling price is $95,000, and costs to complete and sell are $22,000. Its carrying amount is:",
 "$73,000", ["$80,000", "$95,000", "$70,000"],
 "FIFO inventory is carried at the lower of cost and net realizable value. NRV is $95,000 - $22,000 = $73,000, below cost, so inventory is written down $7,000."),
("The lower of cost or market rule, rather than lower of cost and NRV, still applies to inventory measured using:",
 "LIFO or the retail method", ["FIFO or average cost", "Specific identification only", "Every costing method"],
 "ASU 2015-11 moved FIFO and average cost to lower of cost and net realizable value. LIFO and the retail inventory method keep lower of cost or market."),
("A LIFO item costs $55. Replacement cost is $52, net realizable value is $60 and NRV less a normal profit is $48. The item is carried at:",
 "$52", ["$55", "$60", "$48"],
 "Market is the middle of replacement cost, the ceiling (NRV) and the floor (NRV less normal profit), so $52. Lower of $55 cost and $52 market is $52."),
("Goods shipped FOB shipping point on December 29 arrive at the buyer on January 3. At December 31 they belong in:",
 "The buyer's inventory", ["The seller's inventory", "Neither party's inventory", "Both, split evenly"],
 "Under FOB shipping point, title passes when the goods leave the seller, so goods in transit belong to the buyer at year-end."),
("Company A ships goods on consignment to Company B, which has not yet sold them at year-end. The goods are included in:",
 "Company A's inventory", ["Company B's inventory", "Neither company's inventory", "B's, as a receivable"],
 "The consignor keeps title until the consignee sells to a customer, so unsold consigned goods stay in the consignor's inventory."),
("Beginning inventory was $140,000, purchases $600,000, freight-in $15,000 and purchase returns $10,000. Cost of goods sold was $590,000. Ending inventory is:",
 "$155,000", ["$140,000", "$165,000", "$125,000"],
 "Goods available: $140,000 + $600,000 + $15,000 - $10,000 = $745,000. Less COGS of $590,000 leaves $155,000."),
("A perpetual inventory ledger shows $412,000, but the year-end count supports $405,000. The company should record:",
 "A $7,000 debit to cost of sales", ["A $7,000 credit to cost of sales", "A $7,000 debit to inventory", "No entry until next year"],
 "Shrinkage is recorded by crediting inventory $7,000 and debiting cost of goods sold (or a shrinkage expense), so the ledger agrees with the count."),
]
